  "content": {
    "id": "p9-task-45-48-content",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "45",
    "prompt": "Find the equation of the exponential function $y=k(a^x)$ where $a>0$, that passes through the following points.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p9-part-45",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$(1,4)$ and $(4,32)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y=2\\times2^x$",
          "worked": "Substitute the two points into $y=ka^x$.\n$$\\begin{align*}ka&=4\\\\ka^4&=32\\\\a^3&=\\frac{ka^4}{ka}\\\\a^3&=\\frac{32}{4}\\\\a^3&=8\\\\a&=2\\\\k&=\\frac{4}{2}\\\\k&=2\\\\y&=2\\times2^x\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 86
      },
      {
        "id": "p9-part-46",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$(1,2)$ and $(4,16)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y=2^x$",
          "worked": "Substitute the two points into $y=ka^x$.\n$$\\begin{align*}ka&=2\\\\ka^4&=16\\\\a^3&=\\frac{ka^4}{ka}\\\\a^3&=\\frac{16}{2}\\\\a^3&=8\\\\a&=2\\\\k&=\\frac{2}{2}\\\\k&=1\\\\y&=2^x\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 86
      },
      {
        "id": "p9-part-47",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$(0,2)$ and $(2,18)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y=2\\times3^x$",
          "worked": "The point $(0,2)$ gives the multiplier.\n$$\\begin{align*}k&=2\\\\2a^2&=18\\\\a^2&=9\\\\a&=3\\\\y&=2\\times3^x\\end{align*}$$\nChoose the positive root because $a>0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 60
      },
      {
        "id": "p9-part-48",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$(1,20)$ and $(-1,0.8)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p9-part-48-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "y=4\\times5^x",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p9-part-48-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Divide the point equations to obtain ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "a^2=25",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "; use the positive base.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Use $a^{-1}=\\frac{1}{a}$ and write $0.8=\\frac{4}{5}$.\n$$\\begin{align*}ka&=20\\\\\\frac{k}{a}&=\\frac45\\\\a^2&=\\frac{ka}{k/a}\\\\a^2&=\\frac{20}{4/5}\\\\a^2&=25\\\\a&=5\\\\k&=\\frac{20}{5}\\\\k&=4\\\\y&=4\\times5^x\\end{align*}$$\nChoose the positive root because $a>0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 96
      }
    ]
  },
